1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:00,930
So, um,

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:05,940
imagine your ministry spends $50 million on a new digital ID system.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,440
Okay. And then, you know, you find out the Department of Education just spent forty million building the exact same thing next door.

4
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:14,580
Yeah. I mean, it happens all the time. It is a classic problem.

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:15,260
It really is.

6
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:22,900
So today, we are doing a deep dive into how you actually stop this massive duplication.

7
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:29,820
Our mission is to explore how large government digital systems can be upgraded without becoming completely siloed.

8
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:36,680
Exactly. And we are looking at a framework called PAERA, the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture.

9
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:41,720
Right. And specifically, we are focusing on phase three, which is known as Adapt.

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,900
We will use a demonstration country called ProgresA to see how this actually works on the ground.

11
00:00:46,940 --> 00:00:48,900
So this is where the theory hits reality, right?

12
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:52,660
Because to avoid that duplicated spending, your program needs a master blueprint.

13
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:53,940
Right, an enterprise architecture.

14
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,660
But, um, you cannot just drop a generic blueprint onto a government.

15
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:04,650
During the Adapt phase, architects take the PAERA framework and fit it to ProgresA's specific needs.

16
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:07,100
Without breaking the overarching logic.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,540
But wait, if your minister has to adjust the rules anyway,

18
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:14,140
why not just write new principles from scratch?

19
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,920
Wouldn't you want them to perfectly match your local terrain?

20
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:23,880
Well, uh, you would think so, but drafting from scratch just takes months of bureaucratic arguing.

21
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:24,460
Oh, I bet.

22
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:30,510
Yeah. It is highly inefficient. Instead, you inherit the foundational thinking, and you just localize the wording.

23
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:38,130
For instance, ProgresA takes the baseline once only principle and points it directly at their local data protection act.

24
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:42,200
Okay, so they keep the strong foundation but make it legally binding locally.

25
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:44,580
Exactly, and they add necessary local context.

26
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:47,830
Yeah. Like, uh, principle for offline access in rural schools.

27
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:50,580
Plus, every principle gets a written implication.

28
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:56,380
A written implication. So if an agency tries to ignore a rule later- Mm. -the board has a written standard to settle the argument.

29
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:58,780
Precisely. It keeps things from getting fragmented.

30
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:03,380
Okay, so once the rules are localized, how does your ministry actually get the technology?

31
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,880
Like, how do they acquire the digital building blocks?

32
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:12,020
So looking at the blueprints, they use a deliberate matrix of four choices for sourcing.

33
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:13,140
You have build,

34
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:15,780
buy, share and sandbox.

35
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,660
Got it. So four options. Let us walk through those.

36
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,540
How do they decide what to build?

37
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:23,540
Well, they build things like the learner register.

38
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:25,980
Right, because that is the authoritative core.

39
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,100
Nobody else can own that data.

40
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:31,220
Exactly. But then you have things like identity and data exchange.

41
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:32,549
For those-

42
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:33,660
They share them.

43
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:35,740
Right. They choose to share. ProgresS

44
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:39,140
consumes the national identity authority's platform.

45
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:41,530
They never mint a second identity.

46
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:46,440
Which is brilliant, because otherwise, data just ends up being rekeyed across duplicated systems.

47
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:49,620
Right, and they also share the digital government authority as backbone.

48
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:54,010
Oh, okay. But what about features that are not totally unique to government, like exam management?

49
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:56,060
You would not build that from scratch, right?

50
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:01,540
No, you buy it. It is a solved problem. There are mature products on the market, so you just purchase a license.

51
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:05,359
Building that custom would just leave you locked in- Yeah. -to one supplier.

52
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,420
Or make things really costly to change later.

53
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:11,620
That makes total sense. So what about the fourth option, the sandbox?

54
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:17,460
Ah, right. Sandbox is for wildcard situations, like a brand-new analytics capability.

55
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:23,240
Nobody is entirely sure how it will integrate, so it must be tested before committing.

56
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:25,600
So you prove it works without risking the budget.

57
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:29,240
So looking at these four options, is there a default?

58
00:03:29,370 --> 00:03:31,720
The default is always to reuse and share.

59
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:37,109
Always. Okay. Building custom is an exception that requires serious justification,

60
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:43,500
and securing those whole of government savings requires a sector-wide architect, not just a procurement rule.

61
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:46,440
Right, to stop individual agencies from just going rogue.

62
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:49,500
And this whole Adapt phase is actually surprisingly fast, right?

63
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:50,780
Like four to six weeks.

64
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:53,160
Yeah. But it ends with a very consequential gate.

65
00:03:53,840 --> 00:04:00,300
The board officially signs off on the localized principles in that specific build, buy, share sandbox matrix.

66
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:06,890
So if you skip that final sign-off, every individual project just relitigates the build versus reuse debate all over again.

67
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:11,640
Exactly. Which causes the exact duplicated systems this architecture exists to prevent.

68
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:15,400
Right. That sign-off legally commits the agencies to cooperation.

